# Copy this file to .env and replace every placeholder. Never commit .env.
# Local and staging environments use generated fake data only (CLAUDE.md).

# Local PostgreSQL container (read by `docker compose up -d db`)
POSTGRES_USER=gliagora
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=change-me
POSTGRES_DB=gliagora

# Connection string used by the app and the Payload CLI.
# User, password and database must match the three values above.
DATABASE_URL=postgres://gliagora:change-me@localhost:5432/gliagora

# Signs Payload admin sessions. Generate one with: openssl rand -hex 32
PAYLOAD_SECRET=replace-with-a-long-random-string

# Signs the spam-check (ALTCHA) challenges of the public forms. At least 32 characters; generate
# one with: openssl rand -hex 32. Without it the "Suggest a listing" form says it is temporarily
# unavailable (the rest of the site works).
ALTCHA_HMAC_KEY=replace-with-openssl-rand-hex-32

# The site's own address (https://…, no trailing slash): the only origin whose requests may use an
# editor's session cookie, and the start of links in Payload's own emails. The production server
# sets it from SITE_DOMAIN (deploy/compose.prod.yml). Leave it empty locally and in CI, so the app
# and its admin work on any port.
SERVER_URL=

# The site's stage (D41, D50): `private` (the default, the full site as built) or `m2`, the M2
# state: the public pages (src/lib/public-pages.ts) in their M2 versions, with the reduced header
# and footer, the coming-soon /en/ and robots.txt and sitemaps with only those pages. Caddy reads
# the same value on the server. Read at request time, so restart the app after changing it.
# SITE_STAGE=private

# Scheduled jobs (the nightly ROR import, the hourly expiry of listings, the retention purges and
# the automatic emails) run only when this is true: set it on the production server only
# (deploy/compose.prod.yml does). Leave it false locally and in CI; run them once with
# `pnpm jobs:run`.
PAYLOAD_JOBS_AUTORUN=false

# Outgoing email (the outcome of a suggestion, the acknowledgement of a report). Without SMTP_HOST
# nothing is sent and no address is logged. Locally, send to Mailpit, which keeps every message on
# this computer (`docker compose --profile mail up -d mailpit`, then open http://127.0.0.1:8025):
# SMTP_HOST=127.0.0.1
# SMTP_PORT=1025
# SMTP_SECURE=false
# SMTP_USER=
# SMTP_PASS=
# EMAIL_FROM=Gliagora <hello@gliagora.com>
# The cap on the emails the public forms send in all, an hour and a day (empty: 50 and 200; the
# server sets it from the mailbox's sending limit, deploy/.env.example). Never above a value set
# here: the hour is never above the day, and a value the app cannot read is replaced by the
# stricter of its default and the other setting.
# EMAIL_CAP_HOUR=
# EMAIL_CAP_DAY=

# listmonk, the newsletter service (M2), for trying it locally: `docker compose --profile newsletter
# up -d listmonk` starts it at http://127.0.0.1:9000/admin/ (user gliagora-admin with
# LISTMONK_ADMIN_PASSWORD, at least 8 characters, made at its first start; without it, the first
# visit makes the account), connected as its own database user `listmonk` with
# LISTMONK_DB_PASSWORD (any long random text; required by that profile), never as the superuser
# above. Then `pnpm listmonk:setup` applies the server's settings to LISTMONK_DATABASE_URL (its
# database in the PostgreSQL above, with that superuser) and makes the app's API user with
# LISTMONK_API_TOKEN (any long random text). LISTMONK_URL and the same token are what the app will
# use for the newsletter signup. LISTMONK_BIN is a listmonk program file (the release's linux
# binary): `pnpm listmonk:setup` then installs listmonk itself, and the database tests
# (pnpm test:db) start it when Docker is not available.
# LISTMONK_DB_PASSWORD=
# LISTMONK_ADMIN_PASSWORD=
# LISTMONK_DATABASE_URL=postgres://gliagora:change-me@localhost:5432/listmonk
# LISTMONK_API_TOKEN=
# LISTMONK_URL=http://127.0.0.1:9000
# LISTMONK_BIN=/path/to/listmonk

# Member sign-in (M4b). Each way of signing in switches on by itself: Google with both GOOGLE_*
# settings, ORCID with both ORCID_* settings, email links with SMTP_HOST and ALTCHA_HMAC_KEY.
# Without any, signing in is off. Local development and the tests use fake providers instead
# (below); real keys are for the production server only.
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=
# ORCID_CLIENT_ID=
# ORCID_CLIENT_SECRET=
# ORCID's issuer: https://orcid.org by default. Its sandbox (https://sandbox.orcid.org, with a
# sandbox client of your own) works locally only; the production server refuses it.
# ORCID_ISSUER=https://sandbox.orcid.org

# Tests only: the fake Google and ORCID of the end-to-end tests (tests/fake-oidc/server.ts; start
# them with `pnpm fake:oidc`, or let `pnpm test:e2e` start them). Both providers then use the
# issuers <AUTH_TEST_ISSUER>/google and <AUTH_TEST_ISSUER>/orcid with a test client, whatever the
# settings above say. Honoured only for an address on this computer (localhost, 127.0.0.1, [::1])
# and never where PAYLOAD_JOBS_AUTORUN is "true"; the production files never name it. Give the
# app and the tests the same value.
# AUTH_TEST_ISSUER=http://127.0.0.1:9400

# Tests only: what the end-to-end tests read (playwright.config.ts loads this file). MAILPIT_URL is
# Mailpit's address, where the tests read the emails the app sent (with SMTP_HOST above pointing at
# Mailpit); E2E_MEMBER_TOKEN is the value `pnpm e2e:member` made the test members' sessions from
# (any text; the same for both). MAILPIT_BIN is a Mailpit program file the database tests start
# when Docker is not available (pnpm test:db).
# MAILPIT_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8025
# E2E_MEMBER_TOKEN=e2e-test-only
# MAILPIT_BIN=/path/to/mailpit

# Tests only: the M2 state through the production Caddyfile (tests/e2e/m2.spec.ts, the Playwright
# project m2). CADDY_BIN is Caddy 2.11.4, the version deploy/compose.prod.yml pins; M2_APP_URL is a
# second `pnpm start` of the same build with SITE_STAGE=m2 and its own PORT, which must be running
# whenever M2_APP_URL is set (the m2 tests fail without it; the other projects do not wait for it).
# Playwright then starts three Caddies, at these HTTPS ports (each with its plain-HTTP port 100
# above): the M2 state, the private stage and the M2 state with every text opened.
# CADDY_BIN=/path/to/caddy
# M2_APP_URL=http://127.0.0.1:3001
# CADDY_M2_PORT=8443
# CADDY_PRIVATE_PORT=8444
# CADDY_WIDE_PORT=8445

# Optional: a free ROR client ID raises the ROR API rate limit (https://ror.readme.io/docs/client-id).
# ROR_CLIENT_ID=
